WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:04.040
Here is a beautiful hand manipulated lace

2
00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:08.720
pattern that takes time.
But it's so worth the effort.

3
00:00:08.920 --> 00:00:12.080
For this example,
we're working over a multiple of 10

4
00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:18.200
stitches and four rows, but you could vary
this depending on your machine and yarn.

5
00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:21.200
Cast-on and knit a row or two.

6
00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:23.640
End with the carriage at the right.

7
00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:29.240
Remove the yarn from the carriage and
hand knit the stitches of the next row.

8
00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:32.480
In my example, I'm knitting four stitches

9
00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:38.840
normally as if they were stockinette and
then elongating the next six stitches.

10
00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:41.840
Make these stitches as large as possible.

11
00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:44.560
Pull them all the way back.

12
00:00:56.240 --> 00:00:59.360
Knit 4 stitches.

13
00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:09.480
Elongate 6, do this all
the way across the row.

14
00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:20.400
Now your carriage is on the right

15
00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:26.960
and the working yarn is on the left,
so you'll need to free past the carriage.

16
00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:31.280
I'm carefully pulling all
the needles out to hold position.

17
00:01:31.480 --> 00:01:36.440
I need to pull down on the knitting
to keep the big loops on the needles

18
00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:40.680
and keep them from catching
on the gate pegs.

19
00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:47.320
I tried using a transfer tool to add
weight as I'm pulling the needles to hold,

20
00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:51.800
but if you watch closely, you'll
probably see a couple of droppedped stitches.

21
00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:58.480
Now, picking up drop stitches isn't
too bad, but I did find an easier way.

22
00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:15.120
Once all the needles are in hold position,
confirm that all the stitches are

23
00:02:15.320 --> 00:02:19.560
on the needles and no loops
are around the gate pegs.

24
00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:25.000
Set the carriage to hold
and free pass across.

25
00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:37.880
Cancel hold, thread up the carriage

26
00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:45.480
and knit a row left to right across
all the stitches. That's rows 1 and 2.

27
00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:52.280
Row three alternates the enlarged stitches.
I'm making the first five large

28
00:02:52.480 --> 00:03:00.640
knitting 4, making the next 6 large,
knitting 4 all the way across the row.

29
00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:09.320
Now, this time I got a little better
at pulling those needles to hold without

30
00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:12.520
dropping stitches.
Instead of using the tool,

31
00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:16.880
which was a bit clumsy,
I used my fingers and I pinched

32
00:03:17.080 --> 00:03:23.600
the knitting below the needles
as I pulled the needles out.

33
00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:41.640
Again, with all the needles in hold, set

34
00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:46.120
that carriage to hold.
Free pass to the left.

35
00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:52.600
Cancel hold. Thread up the carriage
and knit to the right.

36
00:03:53.320 --> 00:03:58.760
You'll repeat these four rows for the
entire stitch pattern. In this photo

37
00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:01.240
I pulled my knitting out over the ribber

38
00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:05.600
so that you could see the stitch pattern,
but you really don't want to knit this

39
00:04:05.800 --> 00:04:12.480
way, especially because you are elongating
those stitches and hand manipulating.

40
00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:15.680
This is a beautiful stitch pattern

41
00:04:15.880 --> 00:04:22.760
for scarves or shawls, and both sides look
great, you can work it on any machine

42
00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:24.640
and you can also use it as an all-over

43
00:04:24.840 --> 00:04:30.000
design or as an insert for just
a touch of lace in your knitting.

